1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,500
It stands in Southern Egypt, one of the greatest engineering feats of the 20th century.

2
00:00:07,501 --> 00:00:10,300
The largest rock-filled dam in the world.

3
00:00:10,301 --> 00:00:15,700
Two and a half miles long, it harnesses the flow of the fabled River Nile.

4
00:00:15,701 --> 00:00:19,800
Now, the Aswan Dam on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,680
The Aswan High Dam, which controls the River Nile in southern Egypt, is the largest rock-filled

6
00:00:40,681 --> 00:00:42,380
dam in the world.

7
00:00:42,381 --> 00:00:48,120
Yet it is very difficult to get a good look at it.

8
00:00:48,121 --> 00:00:53,380
That's because it's almost impossible to determine where the land ends and the dam begins.

9
00:00:53,381 --> 00:00:58,840
In fact, you can be standing on it and not know it.

10
00:00:58,841 --> 00:01:03,200
The Aswan Dam is that big.

11
00:01:03,201 --> 00:01:06,620
Three years of Nile water is stored behind it.

12
00:01:06,621 --> 00:01:11,000
If released all at once, this water would sweep through the Nile Valley, potentially

13
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:14,660
wiping out the entire country in a day.

14
00:01:14,661 --> 00:01:21,180
The superiority of this engineering is not only in its design, the concept of it, of

15
00:01:21,181 --> 00:01:25,640
its landfill, but also in its enormous size.

16
00:01:25,641 --> 00:01:32,140
It's a massive project, and indeed it is more massive than Sheop's pyramid at Giza in Cairo.

17
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:37,180
The people of Egypt have a saying.

18
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:40,720
The Nile is Egypt, and Egypt is the Nile.

19
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:50,620
Since the beginning of recorded time, the Nile has been the source of life for the land through which it flows.

20
00:01:50,621 --> 00:01:59,120
This is true perhaps most of all in Egypt, a land of relentless desert where rain is sometimes not seen for a decade.

21
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:08,000
The thousand mile course of the Nile through Egypt is a narrow valley of green never more than 12 miles wide

22
00:02:08,001 --> 00:02:12,500
until it reaches Cairo and broadens in the delta to meet the Mediterranean Sea.

23
00:02:12,501 --> 00:02:25,980
These fertile areas make up less than 5% of Egypt's land, yet 96% of her population live here.

24
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:33,480
65 million people living in an area only slightly larger than the state of Maryland.

25
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:40,920
To the people of the Nile Valley, the river is the annual miracle that gives life to their parched and scorched lands.

26
00:02:40,921 --> 00:02:43,920
Egypt does not exist without the Nile.

27
00:02:43,921 --> 00:02:47,920
The Nile is the factor that created Egypt.

28
00:02:47,921 --> 00:02:52,920
And Egyptians are extraordinarily proud of the Nile.

29
00:02:52,921 --> 00:02:57,920
I mean, they view themselves, the Egyptians, as the earliest civilization

30
00:02:57,921 --> 00:03:02,920
in terms of a highly structured government, a highly powerful government,

31
00:03:02,921 --> 00:03:05,920
the accomplishments of the pharaonic civilization.

32
00:03:05,921 --> 00:03:07,920
And all of this was due to the Nile.

33
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:17,920
The ancient Egyptians prayed to Hopi, the god of the river, for the gift of water.

34
00:03:17,921 --> 00:03:24,920
The Nile was what enabled the great stones for the pyramids to be floated down on the flood, you know, to be built.

35
00:03:24,921 --> 00:03:27,920
But it's essentially the agriculture.

36
00:03:27,921 --> 00:03:33,920
Without that, you could not have had the extraordinary productivity and fertility of soil

37
00:03:33,921 --> 00:03:40,920
That enabled Egypt for millennia not only to produce enough food for themselves, but to export food.

38
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:47,920
But nature can be fickle. Some years the Nile's gifts weren't so welcome.

39
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:54,920
Throughout the entire history of Egypt, ancient, Coptic and Islamic,

40
00:03:54,921 --> 00:03:55,700
Islamic.

41
00:03:55,701 --> 00:04:03,400
You get all of these stories of what happened when Egypt underwent either flood

42
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:05,749
or drought.

43
00:04:05,750 --> 00:04:11,400
These are very common stories that are told not only through the books of religion,

44
00:04:11,401 --> 00:04:12,928
but also through myth.

45
00:04:12,929 --> 00:04:17,720
And we see from these stories the tremendous suffering that Egyptians

46
00:04:17,721 --> 00:04:19,014
went through.

47
00:04:19,015 --> 00:04:26,580
As far back as 3,000 years BC, the pharaohs built levees to protect farmland

48
00:04:26,581 --> 00:04:27,491
from flooding.

49
00:04:27,492 --> 00:04:32,760
Barrages or barriers were erected in the river to divert the flow into irrigation

50
00:04:32,761 --> 00:04:38,683
canals and ditches. The early Egyptians were accomplished water managers.

51
00:04:38,684 --> 00:04:39,900
They learned to

52
00:04:39,901 --> 00:04:44,100
build low mud walls to hold the water for longer periods in shallow basins.

53
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:54,720
Needing to make water in effect run uphill, they devised methods like the water wheel, which are still in use today.

54
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:05,460
The Nile carried sediment called silt all the way from the highlands of Ethiopia.

55
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:14,560
During a normal flood season when the Nile overcame its banks, it deposited this rich fertilizer onto the farmland.

56
00:05:14,561 --> 00:05:17,800
When the water receded, the silt remained.

57
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:21,960
Combined with enough water and given Egypt's year-round sunshine,

58
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:26,160
this silt made Egypt the richest farmland in the world,

59
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:29,560
capable of yielding three crops a year.

60
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:36,980
But that was in a good year, and there were many bad ones.

61
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:39,860
For thousands of years of Egyptian history,

62
00:05:39,861 --> 00:05:44,160
there was no way to save the water of the good year for the salvation of the bad.

63
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:49,460
Once the precious Nile water escaped into the Mediterranean, it was gone forever.

64
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:55,280
Since 1517, the Ottoman Turks had ruled Egypt.

65
00:05:56,080 --> 00:06:02,140
In 1805, Muhammad Ali the Great, father of modern Egypt, became viceroy to the Turkish sultan.

66
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,300
The Egyptian leader was ambitious, looking for new ways to enrich his country.

67
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:12,420
His French advisor suggested he plant cotton and sell the harvest to Europe.

68
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:17,800
This advice would seem particularly shrewd a half century later,

69
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:22,800
when the cotton crop of the southern United States was blockaded during the American Civil War,

70
00:06:23,580 --> 00:06:26,280
allowing Egypt to corner the world cotton market.

71
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:33,200
The new crop required water in the late spring and summer when the Nile is low.

72
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:37,360
Egypt needed a method of retaining the waters from the flood season.

73
00:06:37,361 --> 00:06:46,800
Muhammad Ali authorized the construction of a barrage at the apex of the delta, just beyond Cairo,

74
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:51,880
in order to hold a head of water high enough to supply canals and ditches to the north.

75
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:54,780
But he never saw it finished.

76
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:58,980
It was not until the reign of his grandson, Ismail Pasha,

77
00:06:58,981 --> 00:07:03,120
that the project was successfully completed under the supervision of the British,

78
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:05,860
who had a heavy interest in Egypt's cotton crop.

79
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:15,120
By 1890, it could hold a head of 13 feet and was diverting water into the delta.

80
00:07:15,121 --> 00:07:24,240
A lot of the earlier barrages were principally built using large pieces of stone cut from

81
00:07:24,241 --> 00:07:27,460
rocky hills in the surrounding sites.

82
00:07:27,461 --> 00:07:33,140
In fact, the ancient Egyptians built most of their temples from exactly the same kind

83
00:07:33,141 --> 00:07:34,140
of stone.

84
00:07:34,141 --> 00:07:41,140
By the end of the 19th century, the relatively new building material, reinforced concrete,

85
00:07:41,141 --> 00:07:45,140
was revolutionizing bridge and dam construction all over the world.

86
00:07:45,141 --> 00:07:52,140
Egypt's eternal dream of damming the Nile was now, for the first time, within the realm of possibility.

87
00:07:52,141 --> 00:07:58,140
The site chosen for this first dam was at Aswan, a small trading town in Upper Egypt.

88
00:07:58,141 --> 00:08:01,140
Here, the Nile narrows and picks up speed.

89
00:08:01,141 --> 00:08:09,900
The Aswan Dam itself was then built later during the reign of Khedav Tawfiq, who governed

90
00:08:09,901 --> 00:08:12,780
Egypt during the latter part of the 19th century.

91
00:08:12,781 --> 00:08:17,660
It was built with British help in terms of its engineering, but it was also a project

92
00:08:17,661 --> 00:08:24,220
whose architecture is very similar to the kind of projects that you see all over Europe

93
00:08:24,221 --> 00:08:30,340
and on rivers built around the early part of the 19th century.

94
00:08:30,341 --> 00:08:35,880
The original Aswan Dam, the Low Dam as it's commonly known today, stretched two miles

95
00:08:35,881 --> 00:08:40,680
across the valley and rose nearly 90 feet above the riverbed.

96
00:08:40,681 --> 00:08:45,520
It was designed to allow the bulk of the annual flood to flow freely through its gates.

97
00:08:45,521 --> 00:08:50,960
Then, in the last stage of the flood, the gates were closed, holding back just enough

98
00:08:50,961 --> 00:08:56,880
of the water to re-irrigate the fields later in the summer.

99
00:08:56,881 --> 00:09:01,720
The Low Dam increased the productivity of Egypt's arable land, but it was neither big

100
00:09:01,721 --> 00:09:06,160
enough nor strong enough to store more than a year's supply of water.

101
00:09:06,161 --> 00:09:14,880
And on the African continent, droughts lasting several years or more are not uncommon.

102
00:09:14,881 --> 00:09:20,440
The success of the Low Dam was a boon to Egypt, but it only provided temporary relief from

103
00:09:20,441 --> 00:09:23,300
the dilemma that had haunted her always.

104
00:09:23,301 --> 00:09:30,300
the new century would find that dilemma becoming ever more critical.

105
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:37,139
The Aswan High Dam has permitted the reclamation of one million acres of desert land.

106
00:09:37,140 --> 00:09:37,840
The Aswan

107
00:09:37,841 --> 00:09:40,705
Dam will continue on Modern Marvels.

108
00:09:40,706 --> 00:09:44,740
We now return to the Aswan Dam on Modern Marvels.

109
00:09:44,741 --> 00:09:53,180
During the early part of the 19th century, in the reign of Muhammad Ali, there was a

110
00:09:53,181 --> 00:09:56,920
cropped acre of land for every inhabitant of Egypt.

111
00:09:56,921 --> 00:10:01,620
By the end of his grandson Ismail's reign a few decades later, that figurehead dropped

112
00:10:01,621 --> 00:10:04,580
to three quarters of an acre.

113
00:10:04,581 --> 00:10:10,300
Once known as the breadbasket of civilization for the abundant surplus of her harvest, now

114
00:10:10,301 --> 00:10:13,920
Egypt struggled to feed her own.

115
00:10:13,921 --> 00:10:18,420
Even with every inch of workable farmland in Egypt planted and producing crops, the

116
00:10:18,421 --> 00:10:20,960
country was losing ground.

117
00:10:20,961 --> 00:10:26,260
The population was increasing rapidly, while the food supply remained static.

118
00:10:26,261 --> 00:10:32,420
For centuries, Egypt had been ruled or occupied by foreign powers.

119
00:10:32,421 --> 00:10:39,080
Greeks, Romans, Arabs, Turks, all grew rich exploiting her limited resources, while Egypt

120
00:10:39,081 --> 00:10:42,820
herself remained hamstrung by poverty.

121
00:10:42,821 --> 00:10:47,920
By the end of the 19th century, Egypt was deep in debt to European bankers.

122
00:10:47,921 --> 00:10:51,900
Her cotton crop was heavily mortgaged, and she had been forced to sell her shares in

123
00:10:51,901 --> 00:10:57,220
the Suez Canal, the most profitable waterway in the hemisphere, which her people had labored

124
00:10:57,221 --> 00:11:01,060
so long to build.

125
00:11:01,061 --> 00:11:04,640
The canal was jointly owned by the French and the British.

126
00:11:04,641 --> 00:11:09,800
In 1882, in response to some nationalistic rumblings in Egypt, Great Britain sent an

127
00:11:09,801 --> 00:11:13,753
army to occupy the country and protect their investment.

128
00:11:13,754 --> 00:11:15,800
They were to remain there for

129
00:11:15,801 --> 00:11:17,396
nearly 75 years.

130
00:11:17,397 --> 00:11:24,880
In 1936, Egypt was granted her independence from the Turks, but the British

131
00:11:24,881 --> 00:11:25,581
remained.

132
00:11:25,582 --> 00:11:32,040
The first 50 years of the 20th century saw a steady increase in the Egyptian population.

133
00:11:32,840 --> 00:11:37,640
By 1950, there were only two cropped acres for every seven people.

134
00:11:38,740 --> 00:11:40,900
Egypt was declining year after year,

135
00:11:41,180 --> 00:11:44,400
until now it stood as one of the poorest nations in the world,

136
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:46,740
and getting poorer every day.

137
00:11:47,780 --> 00:11:51,520
Egyptians throughout the 20th century were always of the belief

138
00:11:51,521 --> 00:11:56,020
that they have been held back because of the issues relating principally

139
00:11:56,021 --> 00:11:59,540
to the inability to control the flow of water in the Nile.

140
00:11:59,541 --> 00:12:01,540
And as such, it was always a dream.

141
00:12:01,541 --> 00:12:05,860
People talked about it in novels, they talked about it in literature, they talked about

142
00:12:05,861 --> 00:12:07,800
it in popular culture.

143
00:12:07,801 --> 00:12:12,940
The notion of being able to solve this problem was so much on the minds of Egyptians, and

144
00:12:12,941 --> 00:12:17,800
it was certainly on the minds of the Egyptian officers who took control of the Egyptian

145
00:12:17,801 --> 00:12:22,260
government in the early 50s as part of the Egyptian revolution.

146
00:12:22,261 --> 00:12:30,260
On July 23, 1952, the Egyptian revolution began with a bloodless coup.

147
00:12:30,261 --> 00:12:37,260
A group of army officers calling themselves Free Officers seized power, sending King Farouk

148
00:12:37,261 --> 00:12:40,260
and his family into exile.

149
00:12:40,261 --> 00:12:45,260
On the day he took power, the leader of the Free Officers, Gamal Abdel Nasser, became

150
00:12:45,261 --> 00:12:50,260
the first true Egyptian to rule his country since the last Syed Pharaoh submitted to the

151
00:12:50,261 --> 00:12:54,260
admitted to the Persians in the middle of the 6th century BC.

152
00:12:54,261 --> 00:13:00,260
The new young regime looked around for a symbol, a rallying point with which to unite Egyptians

153
00:13:00,261 --> 00:13:05,260
and instill confidence in the world community that the nation was in capable hands.

154
00:13:05,261 --> 00:13:08,260
Within two weeks, they had found their cause.

155
00:13:08,261 --> 00:13:13,260
It was an engineering project that had been lying dormant in some bureaucratic cubbyhole,

156
00:13:13,261 --> 00:13:19,900
a highly ambitious plan for a world-class dam to be built at Aswan, within sight of the low dam.

157
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:27,380
The high dam would be the first great step forward into the progressive future Nasser had promised the people.

158
00:13:28,840 --> 00:13:32,560
The Revolutionary Command Council, but particularly Gamal Abdel Nasser, said,

159
00:13:32,561 --> 00:13:36,314
we're going to do this. This will symbolize the new Egypt.

160
00:13:36,315 --> 00:13:38,580
This will symbolize our revolution.

161
00:13:38,581 --> 00:13:47,300
and most important, this will give to us the security that Egypt needs and does not have.

162
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:53,340
As the plans were developed, it became apparent that the new high dam would take 10 years to build.

163
00:13:54,040 --> 00:13:57,300
And although it could potentially double Egypt's agricultural output,

164
00:13:57,860 --> 00:14:03,840
that increase would be effectively canceled out by the increase in population during the years it took to build it.

165
00:14:03,841 --> 00:14:09,120
But this was still good news.

166
00:14:09,121 --> 00:14:14,240
For the first time since the beginning of the 20th century, Egypt would at least not

167
00:14:14,241 --> 00:14:15,520
be getting poorer.

168
00:14:15,521 --> 00:14:21,400
An even more exciting prospect was the potential of the new dam to generate hydroelectric

169
00:14:21,401 --> 00:14:22,720
power.

170
00:14:22,721 --> 00:14:29,300
Enough power to light up 20,000 rural Egyptian villages that had never before had electricity.

171
00:14:29,301 --> 00:14:34,480
That same power could be used to industrialize, provide jobs and manufacture products to sell

172
00:14:34,481 --> 00:14:36,980
on the world market.

173
00:14:36,981 --> 00:14:43,240
This was to be the tremendous symbol of regeneration of Egypt in terms of agriculture, expanding

174
00:14:43,241 --> 00:14:47,560
more agricultural land, increasing productivity.

175
00:14:47,561 --> 00:14:49,960
But it also meant industrial production.

176
00:14:49,961 --> 00:14:55,540
You would get more energy, electrical energy from the power surges through the dam.

177
00:14:55,541 --> 00:15:01,460
This supposedly would lead to an industrial growth that Egypt had never dreamed of before.

178
00:15:03,620 --> 00:15:07,740
Nasser declared that nothing would be allowed to stand in the way of the high dam.

179
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:10,660
It became known as Nasser's Pyramid.

180
00:15:12,600 --> 00:15:17,600
At this stage, the Egyptian leader received a well-timed gift from an unlikely source.

181
00:15:18,260 --> 00:15:25,600
Earlier in 1952, West Germany had paid Israel 3 billion marks in reparation for Jewish suffering under Hitler.

182
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:30,040
When the Arab states complained that these funds would serve to strengthen their enemy,

183
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:32,760
West Germany offered this consolation.

184
00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:38,360
A team of top German engineering experts would work up proposals for the design,

185
00:15:38,780 --> 00:15:42,160
construction, and financing of the Aswan High Dam.

186
00:15:42,161 --> 00:15:48,820
Egypt accepted the offer gratefully and then set out to raise financing for the project.

187
00:15:48,821 --> 00:15:53,195
Where do you find the cash? You go to those people that have it.

188
00:15:53,196 --> 00:15:57,980
And they happen to be in the West, and particularly the United States.

189
00:15:58,700 --> 00:16:08,660
And the cash, as we all know, in the United States resides largely at the World Bank, which is on Pennsylvania Avenue, just down from the White House.

190
00:16:08,661 --> 00:16:16,760
The World Bank may have been eager to loan Egypt the money for the high dam, but their

191
00:16:16,761 --> 00:16:20,420
most influential member had strong reservations.

192
00:16:20,421 --> 00:16:25,900
The United States, which in effect controlled or at least heavily influenced the world lenders,

193
00:16:25,901 --> 00:16:32,100
did not approve of what it considered Nasser's left-leaning social programs or his cozy relations

194
00:16:32,101 --> 00:16:34,460
with the Soviet Union.

195
00:16:34,461 --> 00:16:38,740
This was in the midst of the Cold War, and the Western powers were not anxious to further

196
00:16:38,741 --> 00:16:43,740
empower what they considered a candidate for the Soviet bloc.

197
00:16:43,741 --> 00:16:48,800
The design phase took two years.

198
00:16:48,801 --> 00:16:52,960
Once the German designs were complete, the World Bank sent experts to judge whether or

199
00:16:52,961 --> 00:16:57,220
not the dam made sense economically for Egypt and her investors.

200
00:16:57,221 --> 00:17:01,380
That process took another two years.

201
00:17:01,381 --> 00:17:05,380
By 1956, the World Bank had decided to back the project.

202
00:17:06,380 --> 00:17:08,880
But complications arose in the meantime.

203
00:17:09,380 --> 00:17:14,580
When fighting with Israel broke out, Nasser asked the West for arms, but was rejected.

204
00:17:15,380 --> 00:17:20,580
He turned then to the Soviets, who brokered an arms deal with Czechoslovakia.

205
00:17:22,380 --> 00:17:27,980
Then Nasser angered Western capitalists further by officially recognizing Communist China.

206
00:17:27,981 --> 00:17:33,320
Nasser had never liked the terms of the loan the World Bank was offering.

207
00:17:33,940 --> 00:17:37,560
He thought they gave the West too much control over Egypt's internal affairs.

208
00:17:38,440 --> 00:17:42,620
But now his advisors were telling him to accept the offer while it was still on the table.

209
00:17:43,780 --> 00:17:48,780
Nasser accepted the offer only to be informed by Secretary of State John Foster Dulles

210
00:17:48,781 --> 00:17:50,700
that the offer had been withdrawn.

211
00:17:50,701 --> 00:17:58,460
Angry and humiliated, Nasser announced that he had an alternative plan for the financing of the dam.

212
00:17:59,240 --> 00:18:04,800
On July 26, 1956, he announced the nationalization of the Suez Canal.

213
00:18:05,620 --> 00:18:10,060
The Aswan High Dam, Nasser said, would be built with the profits from the canal.

214
00:18:10,061 --> 00:18:21,280
When the canal was nationalized in 1956, it was nationalized only a few years before it was to revert to Egyptian control.

215
00:18:21,880 --> 00:18:28,760
Because the lease was for 99 years, from 1869 technically until 1968.

216
00:18:28,761 --> 00:18:37,715
It's always forgotten that Egypt had promised to compensate the shareholders, principally the British and the French, from the income of the canal for all of their

217
00:18:37,716 --> 00:18:38,416
shares.

218
00:18:38,417 --> 00:18:45,480
Egypt in fact did abide by this promise and by 1963 Egypt had paid the

219
00:18:45,481 --> 00:18:48,600
shareholders what it owed

220
00:18:49,260 --> 00:18:54,960
seizing the Suez Canal for Egypt was more than a money issue it sent a signal

221
00:18:54,961 --> 00:19:01,220
to the world that Egypt was resolved in its new course of self-determination

222
00:19:01,221 --> 00:19:06,940
France and Great Britain reacted angrily sending troops to invade Egypt and take

223
00:19:06,941 --> 00:19:08,940
back the canal.

224
00:19:11,940 --> 00:19:18,940
It aroused the animosity of the British who had just signed the treaty to evacuate the canal with Nasser

225
00:19:18,941 --> 00:19:22,940
and thought he had planned to do this ahead of time, which he hadn't.

226
00:19:22,941 --> 00:19:27,940
And of course the Israelis were afraid that Nasser was going to start buying weapons,

227
00:19:27,941 --> 00:19:30,940
which he had already done from the Czechs.

228
00:19:30,941 --> 00:19:35,940
So the three of them came together and decided to invade Egypt, which they did in 1956.

229
00:19:35,941 --> 00:19:40,940
in 1956, England, France, and Israel.

230
00:19:40,941 --> 00:19:46,940
Eisenhower deplored this and stopped them and forced them to withdraw from the territories

231
00:19:46,941 --> 00:19:47,940
they had occupied.

232
00:19:47,941 --> 00:19:52,940
Russia and America both stepped in and said, enough is enough, out you go.

233
00:19:52,941 --> 00:19:58,940
But the canal was already nationalized, and Egypt used the funds of that canal to help

234
00:19:58,941 --> 00:20:03,940
build the high dam in Aswan.

235
00:20:03,941 --> 00:20:10,100
Egypt had few friends left in the world when a new offer of financing appeared in October of 1958.

236
00:20:10,980 --> 00:20:13,220
The Soviets were proposing to build the dam.

237
00:20:14,100 --> 00:20:18,740
Their deal contained more strings than the Western offer, but their proposal was long gone.

238
00:20:19,620 --> 00:20:24,100
The Russians were willing to help with the dam, but only if it were seen as a Soviet triumph.

239
00:20:25,300 --> 00:20:32,740
Built by Russian engineers with Russian equipment, the Soviets meant to keep the admiration and acclaim for themselves.

240
00:20:32,741 --> 00:20:35,500
But Nasser would get his high dam.

241
00:20:36,780 --> 00:20:41,020
We now return to the Aswan Dam on Modern Marvels.

242
00:20:44,520 --> 00:20:48,140
The town of Aswan is over 2,000 years old.

243
00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:54,380
In ancient times, it was a marketplace where goods from all over the African continent were bought and sold.

244
00:20:54,381 --> 00:21:03,000
Now in 1960, Egyptian and Russian engineers came to Aswan to begin work on a dam

245
00:21:03,001 --> 00:21:07,620
that would not only provide complete control of the seasonal and annual flow of the Nile,

246
00:21:08,080 --> 00:21:12,320
but would also be a constant source of electrical power during any one year.

247
00:21:13,440 --> 00:21:19,080
The site selected for the new dam was just four miles south or upstream of the old low dam.

248
00:21:20,220 --> 00:21:23,020
The riverbanks there were both solid granite.

249
00:21:23,021 --> 00:21:30,020
The dam had to be built somewhere south of the old small Aswan Dam.

250
00:21:30,021 --> 00:21:39,020
Principally because of the fact that this is the area where you're almost at the southern tip of Egypt.

251
00:21:39,021 --> 00:21:42,020
That's from where you can control most of the flow of the Nile.

252
00:21:42,021 --> 00:21:49,020
Additionally, that's also the area that would allow you to build or construct a lake behind the dam

253
00:21:49,021 --> 00:21:53,520
the dam that would have the least disastrous effects.

254
00:21:53,521 --> 00:21:56,860
That area was the least cultivable area,

255
00:21:56,861 --> 00:22:02,360
and it's an area that is principally a desert landscape.

256
00:22:02,361 --> 00:22:05,560
On the negative side was the condition of the river bottom,

257
00:22:05,561 --> 00:22:08,840
a soft mass of silt and sandy deposits.

258
00:22:08,841 --> 00:22:11,580
This base would have to be solidified to prevent water

259
00:22:11,581 --> 00:22:15,020
from seeping under the future dam.

260
00:22:15,021 --> 00:22:17,680
The German designers had calculated Egypt's annual

261
00:22:17,681 --> 00:22:21,590
water requirements all the way back to the time of the pharaohs.

262
00:22:21,591 --> 00:22:23,300
They determined that Egypt's

263
00:22:23,301 --> 00:22:29,140
current needs could be met by creating a reservoir holding 170 billion cubic meters of water.

264
00:22:30,820 --> 00:22:35,000
Since the bottom of the River Nile was soft and unstable to a great depth,

265
00:22:35,520 --> 00:22:38,988
a conventional concrete structure was out of the question.

266
00:22:38,989 --> 00:22:41,320
The high dam had to be a rock-fill dam,

267
00:22:41,321 --> 00:22:45,480
a hill or ridge in the river made of boulders, gravel and sand.

268
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:49,140
The rock-filled dam is molded to the river bottom,

269
00:22:49,800 --> 00:22:51,860
distributing its weight over a large area

270
00:22:51,861 --> 00:22:55,940
and at no point offering a perpendicular front to the force of the flood.

271
00:22:58,100 --> 00:23:01,780
The plan called for rock-filled coffer or temporary dams

272
00:23:01,781 --> 00:23:03,760
on the upstream and downstream sides.

273
00:23:04,520 --> 00:23:07,400
These would divert the river's flow while the core of the dam was built.

274
00:23:07,401 --> 00:23:13,160
In conventional dams, the coffers are destroyed when the dam is built, but in this case, it

275
00:23:13,161 --> 00:23:18,820
was proposed to mold the upstream and downstream coffers into the main dam and lay a concrete

276
00:23:18,821 --> 00:23:23,400
blanket over them both so that they would form the northern and southern faces of the

277
00:23:23,401 --> 00:23:24,400
dam.

278
00:23:24,401 --> 00:23:31,400
The central core, held firm between the coffers, would be constructed of clay and strengthened

279
00:23:31,401 --> 00:23:36,360
by a horizontal rib of reinforced plastic concrete below the crest on the downstream

280
00:23:36,361 --> 00:23:44,860
The half-mile section of the dam and the river would lie on a thick carpet of dune sand,

281
00:23:44,861 --> 00:23:48,680
compacted by a system of vibration.

282
00:23:48,681 --> 00:23:54,640
It would rise in a slow ascent to reach a peak of 350 feet and then fall gradually away

283
00:23:54,641 --> 00:23:58,660
into the river, half a mile to the north.

284
00:23:58,661 --> 00:24:03,400
The Egyptian, German, and Russian engineers were all fully aware of the consequences should

285
00:24:03,401 --> 00:24:08,360
there be a flaw in the design or construction of the high dam?

286
00:24:08,361 --> 00:24:13,680
The reservoir behind the dam was expected to hold three years worth of Nile water.

287
00:24:13,681 --> 00:24:18,640
If the dam were to break, the force and depth of that water flowing down the Nile would

288
00:24:18,641 --> 00:24:25,460
wipe out the Nile Valley completely, and with it, the 65 million people living there, Egypt

289
00:24:25,461 --> 00:24:29,020
for all intents and purposes, would cease to exist.

290
00:24:29,021 --> 00:24:37,380
A critical question was how to stop water from passing under the dam through the soft, sandy river bottom until it eventually undermined the structure.

291
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:45,100
Since 1952, the German engineers had experimented with the soil and sand of the river bottom.

292
00:24:45,800 --> 00:24:55,099
They determined that the most effective solution would be to create a grout curtain, a mixture of clay and cement injected with hollow drills into the soft sand of the

293
00:24:55,100 --> 00:24:55,800
riverbed.

294
00:24:55,801 --> 00:25:01,900
This would create a wall of artificial rock to a depth of 600 feet below the bottom of the dam.

295
00:25:04,160 --> 00:25:07,620
After the agreement between Egypt and the Soviet Union had been signed,

296
00:25:08,160 --> 00:25:13,020
the Russians took the West German design back to Moscow, where they modified and simplified it.

297
00:25:13,820 --> 00:25:19,380
If the Germans did not have much experience in building dams, they were good engineers and could design one.

298
00:25:20,280 --> 00:25:23,860
The Russians had enormous amount of experience in building dams.

299
00:25:23,861 --> 00:25:29,580
The Russians agreed with the necessity of a grout curtain, but they offered an alternative

300
00:25:29,581 --> 00:25:33,020
to the German plan for water diversion.

301
00:25:33,021 --> 00:25:36,920
The German design called for water tunnels to be built under the dam and under the river

302
00:25:36,921 --> 00:25:40,820
bank, where they would be safe from aerial bombardment.

303
00:25:40,821 --> 00:25:47,620
One of the great concerns that the Egyptians had, and in which they kind of drove the Germans

304
00:25:47,621 --> 00:25:56,320
crazy in the design of the dam was, of course, their great fear, which they had of the Israelis.

305
00:25:56,321 --> 00:26:01,080
And if you're going to build this dam, somebody can blow it up.

306
00:26:01,081 --> 00:26:06,160
There was a great deal of discussion back and forth over this issue, which finally died

307
00:26:06,161 --> 00:26:13,860
away when it became evident that you're going to build a dam which has 42 billion cubic meters

308
00:26:13,861 --> 00:26:20,960
of rock and sand and clay, that the only thing that could ever damage that dam is some monumental

309
00:26:20,961 --> 00:26:22,640
atomic explosion.

310
00:26:25,160 --> 00:26:30,760
The Russian alternative called for just one large diversion channel, 2,000 yards long

311
00:26:30,761 --> 00:26:34,720
and 66 yards wide, to be built in the eastern riverbank.

312
00:26:35,420 --> 00:26:38,280
The hydroelectric station would be built in this channel.

313
00:26:38,281 --> 00:26:45,000
Here, the water would plunge downward, passing through the station's 12 turbines to produce

314
00:26:45,001 --> 00:26:51,000
a maximum output of 2,100 megawatts, one-third more than America's Hoover Dam.

315
00:26:53,800 --> 00:27:02,240
The work began on January 9, 1960, when President Nasser personally ignited 20,000 tons of dynamite

316
00:27:02,241 --> 00:27:04,360
to begin work on the diversion channel.

317
00:27:04,361 --> 00:27:08,680
When the smoke cleared, a new era for Egypt had begun.

318
00:27:10,080 --> 00:27:14,600
The Aswan Dam generates over 10 billion kilowatt hours annually,

319
00:27:14,920 --> 00:27:17,600
enough to provide 50% of Egypt's electricity.

320
00:27:18,640 --> 00:27:22,020
The Aswan Dam will return on Modern Marvels.

321
00:27:27,760 --> 00:27:32,300
The first stage of construction of the Aswan High Dam was the most critical.

322
00:27:33,300 --> 00:27:37,280
The diversion channel had to be completed by the summer of 1964,

323
00:27:38,100 --> 00:27:40,400
before the August flood reached Aswan.

324
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:47,160
Well, when the Russians arrived in 1961, the Egyptian engineers,

325
00:27:48,160 --> 00:27:51,000
and these people have been living on the Nile since the beginning of time,

326
00:27:51,380 --> 00:27:54,480
and they knew exactly its flow and how it operated.

327
00:27:54,780 --> 00:27:57,960
They told the Russians that if we're going to build those cofferdams,

328
00:27:58,220 --> 00:28:01,740
we've got to get them up before we get a high flood.

329
00:28:01,741 --> 00:28:06,640
Otherwise, a high flood will just wash out a half-finished cofferdam.

330
00:28:08,180 --> 00:28:09,680
It was slow going.

331
00:28:10,380 --> 00:28:14,640
Aside from the predictable language and cultural barriers, there was a scarcity of equipment.

332
00:28:15,300 --> 00:28:17,700
And always the oppressive Egyptian heat.

333
00:28:18,880 --> 00:28:22,720
In the summer, the temperature rose to 125 degrees in the shade.

334
00:28:23,560 --> 00:28:27,640
During these months, once they had electric power, work was performed at night.

335
00:28:27,641 --> 00:28:32,640
The Egyptian engineers blamed Russian inefficiency and the failure of Russian equipment.

336
00:28:32,641 --> 00:28:40,640
The Russian equipment were designed for cold weather, and Aswan is one of the hottest places in the world.

337
00:28:40,641 --> 00:28:54,611
The second problem they had was that organizing and getting enormous amounts of equipment from Central Asia and from deep Russia all the way to Aswan was extremely

338
00:28:54,612 --> 00:28:55,640
complicated.

339
00:28:55,641 --> 00:28:58,300
Those routes have never been traversed before.

340
00:28:59,180 --> 00:29:03,200
And the third problem was that who's going to drive it?

341
00:29:04,520 --> 00:29:06,140
A couple of camel boys?

342
00:29:09,300 --> 00:29:15,680
Broken equipment, worn-out drills or trucks with flat tires led to delays in work stoppages.

343
00:29:16,420 --> 00:29:20,660
Squabbling and general discontent led to disorders and strikes at the dam site.

344
00:29:20,661 --> 00:29:26,800
The contract with the Soviets had forbidden any but Russian contractors from working at

345
00:29:26,801 --> 00:29:27,800
the dam.

346
00:29:27,801 --> 00:29:32,580
But now it was becoming obvious to Nasser that the Russians were incapable of meeting

347
00:29:32,581 --> 00:29:34,860
the 1964 deadline.

348
00:29:34,861 --> 00:29:43,740
Over the angry objections of the Soviets, Nasser subcontracted the job to Arab contractors.

349
00:29:43,741 --> 00:29:48,660
The Arabs had experience with large projects, had worked on the Suez Canal and the Cairo

350
00:29:48,661 --> 00:29:49,660
airport.

351
00:29:49,661 --> 00:29:55,020
The new contractors abandoned the faulty Russian equipment in favor of the British tools they

352
00:29:55,021 --> 00:29:57,720
had used successfully in the past.

353
00:29:57,721 --> 00:30:05,680
30,000 Egyptian and Arab laborers were hired at top wages paid in Russian rubles.

354
00:30:05,681 --> 00:30:12,220
The tide began to turn in 1962 as more men and supplies were poured into Aswan.

355
00:30:12,221 --> 00:30:18,860
As work progressed more smoothly and progress could be seen, morale improved.

356
00:30:18,861 --> 00:30:24,020
and Russians were drawn together into a mutual respect, proud to be working on what they

357
00:30:24,021 --> 00:30:26,980
knew was the project of a lifetime.

358
00:30:26,981 --> 00:30:34,640
In May of 1964, the diversion channel and upstream cofferdam was complete with months

359
00:30:34,641 --> 00:30:35,960
to spare.

360
00:30:35,961 --> 00:30:42,320
To the great relief of engineers and politicians alike, President Nasser invited Nikita Khrushchev

361
00:30:42,321 --> 00:30:44,320
to celebrate the event.

362
00:30:44,321 --> 00:30:50,760
With the deadline met, completion of the high dam was now in sight.

363
00:30:50,761 --> 00:30:57,320
The solution to Egypt's age-old problem, the unpredictability of the Nile, was within grasp.

364
00:30:57,321 --> 00:31:03,320
The high dam would increase Egypt's agricultural output by 30%.

365
00:31:03,321 --> 00:31:09,320
The hydroelectric station would provide eight times her current electrical power.

366
00:31:09,321 --> 00:31:12,200
But progress would not come without a price.

367
00:31:13,960 --> 00:31:17,140
The reservoir lake, soon to be created by the high dam,

368
00:31:17,480 --> 00:31:22,440
would eventually extend 300 miles into the desert of Upper Egypt and into the Sudan.

369
00:31:23,780 --> 00:31:29,420
This desert was Nubia, home for millennia to one of the world's oldest and proudest cultures.

370
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:33,500
Nubia is one of the most ancient kingdoms, the old kingdom of Kush.

371
00:31:34,220 --> 00:31:35,787
Nubians have been there. They're not Arabs.

372
00:31:35,788 --> 00:31:37,500
They've been there since the beginning of time.

373
00:31:37,501 --> 00:31:50,077
And this became a very violent issue, vitriolic issue between the Egyptians and the Sudanese until the signing of the Nile Waters Agreement of 1959, which did two

374
00:31:50,078 --> 00:31:50,778
things.

375
00:31:50,779 --> 00:31:54,380
One is it divided the Nile Waters between the Sudan and Egypt.

376
00:31:54,381 --> 00:32:06,873
The second thing it did was it provided, unfortunately, very little compensation from the Egyptians for the relocation of the Nubians, a subject of much bitterness in

377
00:32:06,874 --> 00:32:08,980
the Sudan still to this day.

378
00:32:11,160 --> 00:32:21,880
Amid the rising water, in a 20th century version of Noah's Ark, 117,000 Nubians were relocated hundreds of kilometers from their homeland.

379
00:32:21,881 --> 00:32:28,880
As compensation for their loss, each Nubian family was given a new home, an allowance,

380
00:32:28,881 --> 00:32:31,880
and one to three acres of irrigated land.

381
00:32:31,881 --> 00:32:36,880
And for the first time in their lives, they would have electricity.

382
00:32:40,880 --> 00:32:44,880
But other precious victims remained in the flood's path.

383
00:32:44,881 --> 00:32:51,900
Many ancient Egyptian temples were going to be completely drowned in Lake Nasser.

384
00:32:51,901 --> 00:32:57,520
The most important site was the Abu Simbel Temple.

385
00:32:58,060 --> 00:33:02,420
This was a temple that has always existed on the Nile shores in a peninsula

386
00:33:02,421 --> 00:33:08,600
and had always been a major site that tourists used to come to visit.

387
00:33:10,800 --> 00:33:14,220
The prospect that these temples would be submerged forever

388
00:33:14,221 --> 00:33:17,080
sent a shockwave through the archaeological world.

389
00:33:18,460 --> 00:33:20,940
Once the scope of the situation became known,

390
00:33:21,640 --> 00:33:23,660
UNESCO, the United Nations organization,

391
00:33:24,140 --> 00:33:26,860
sought proposals to rescue 23 of the temples,

392
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:30,440
including the temple of Ramses II at Abu Simbel

393
00:33:30,441 --> 00:33:32,820
and the temple of Philae near Aswan.

394
00:33:33,140 --> 00:33:34,900
Some of them included some crazy things like

395
00:33:34,901 --> 00:33:38,340
we can make a wall of glass and people can go down in elevators

396
00:33:38,341 --> 00:33:40,020
and look at the temples and then return them.

397
00:33:40,440 --> 00:33:41,860
The proposal that was eventually accepted

398
00:33:41,861 --> 00:33:48,240
was to cut the temples into pieces and put them back together higher up on the hill

399
00:33:48,241 --> 00:33:51,360
so that they wouldn't be washed out by the water.

400
00:33:54,480 --> 00:33:58,060
The Temple of Philae was able to be relocated in that manner.

401
00:33:59,640 --> 00:34:05,400
But the temples of Ramses II and his wife, Neferteri, presented an even bigger problem.

402
00:34:06,580 --> 00:34:11,320
These temples were not freestanding, but had been carved out of the mountain behind them.

403
00:34:11,860 --> 00:34:18,540
The effort to save the temples at Abu Simbel was an engineering feat nearly as difficult

404
00:34:18,541 --> 00:34:28,200
and complex as the building of the high dam itself.

405
00:34:28,201 --> 00:34:33,780
Three teams of engineers working day and night for five years moved not only the temples

406
00:34:33,781 --> 00:34:36,540
but part of the mountain they were carved from.

407
00:34:36,541 --> 00:34:47,060
The temple was right there. This is a huge rock. 60 meters below the temple was

408
00:34:47,061 --> 00:34:53,320
located there. Five multinational companies came here and they

409
00:34:53,321 --> 00:34:59,260
started to cut the whole mountain into pieces and they cut the temple into blocks

410
00:34:59,261 --> 00:35:05,660
and they transfer everything 60 meters higher and 200 meters to the back.

411
00:35:05,661 --> 00:35:15,620
This is a safe place because they know that the water of this lake, the highest level will be 183 meters above sea level.

412
00:35:15,840 --> 00:35:22,200
And now the temple is in a safe place because it's on the level 184 above sea level.

413
00:35:22,201 --> 00:35:29,940
Today, the landscape around Lake Nasser is full of relocated temples,

414
00:35:30,600 --> 00:35:37,340
many of which have the stamp of a conservation effort by one particular country or another,

415
00:35:37,820 --> 00:35:42,340
whether we're talking about Germany, Holland, or we're talking about France.

416
00:35:42,900 --> 00:35:46,520
And it's very interesting that these countries take pride in the fact that they contributed

417
00:35:46,521 --> 00:35:51,200
to the building of the Aswanhai Dam, and accordingly, in a sense, to the making of Lake Nasser,

418
00:35:51,201 --> 00:35:54,580
through the preservation effort of these temples.

419
00:35:54,780 --> 00:35:56,180
It's very interesting because it's a twist.

420
00:35:56,540 --> 00:35:59,940
What the West denied Egypt in terms of funding for the building of the dam

421
00:35:59,941 --> 00:36:05,120
ended up going to the preservation of what the West saw as the international heritage,

422
00:36:05,420 --> 00:36:07,860
which is ancient Egyptian civilization and its temples.

423
00:36:10,260 --> 00:36:13,840
The Aswan High Dam was completed in 1970,

424
00:36:14,460 --> 00:36:16,620
ten long years after construction began.

425
00:36:17,440 --> 00:36:20,060
For the first time in its noble and storied history,

426
00:36:20,061 --> 00:36:26,000
the river Nile had been tamed. Egypt was rescued from a century-long spiral into

427
00:36:26,001 --> 00:36:32,760
poverty. The cost had been high, approximately 1.2 billion dollars. It had

428
00:36:32,761 --> 00:36:38,300
also taken a toll in pain and suffering. An estimated 1,000 men lost their lives

429
00:36:38,301 --> 00:36:44,000
through accidents at the site. The dam removed the specter of starvation from

430
00:36:44,001 --> 00:36:49,700
Egypt's foreseeable future, but it was not a perfect solution. 30 years after

431
00:36:49,701 --> 00:36:52,920
completion, its side effects continue to reverberate.

432
00:36:55,360 --> 00:37:01,240
The reservoir created by the Aswan Dam is 300 feet deep, over 300 miles long, and averages

433
00:37:01,241 --> 00:37:02,800
14 miles across.

434
00:37:03,300 --> 00:37:04,720
Modern Marvels will return.

435
00:37:05,700 --> 00:37:09,300
We now return to the Aswan Dam on Modern Marvels.

436
00:37:12,940 --> 00:37:18,320
Nearly a half century ago, a poor nation in the desert made some hard decisions for her

437
00:37:18,321 --> 00:37:19,021
future.

438
00:37:19,600 --> 00:37:22,060
Decisions that would ensure her survival as a nation

439
00:37:22,061 --> 00:37:25,100
and as one of the world's most lasting and treasured cultures.

440
00:37:26,860 --> 00:37:31,360
Today, Egypt is reaping the benefits and the consequences of those decisions.

441
00:37:33,700 --> 00:37:37,960
According to many international organizations and world-renowned scholars,

442
00:37:38,700 --> 00:37:42,560
the Great Dam is considered one of the most important achievements of the 20th century.

443
00:37:44,200 --> 00:37:46,080
Egypt gained a lot out of the Great Dam.

444
00:37:46,081 --> 00:37:50,880
total control of the water of the river and getting our vital needs of water

445
00:37:50,881 --> 00:37:53,700
regardless of the amount of upcoming flooding of the river.

446
00:37:53,701 --> 00:38:02,700
The high dam rescued Egypt from the famine that has wracked much of Africa since 1972.

447
00:38:02,701 --> 00:38:08,760
The dam also protected Egyptian crops and villages from four years of high floods.

448
00:38:08,761 --> 00:38:15,840
As for the hydroelectric station, today it allows Egypt to export surplus power to her Arab neighbors.

449
00:38:15,841 --> 00:38:23,620
Yet as successful as the dam was in solving Egypt's eternal water crisis, there has been

450
00:38:23,621 --> 00:38:25,840
a downside to the high dam.

451
00:38:25,841 --> 00:38:37,640
The high dam meant to Egypt that electrification, increasing water for irrigation, preventing

452
00:38:37,641 --> 00:38:41,060
famine from occurring.

453
00:38:41,061 --> 00:38:45,860
On the negative side, it raised the subsoil water,

454
00:38:45,861 --> 00:38:50,780
which ruined agriculture in the delta.

455
00:38:50,781 --> 00:38:55,160
It increased salination, which ruined the monuments.

456
00:38:55,161 --> 00:38:58,780
It changed the climate, which may be a good thing,

457
00:38:58,781 --> 00:39:02,960
depending on how ecologically minded you are.

458
00:39:02,961 --> 00:39:06,440
So that you have both the for and the against.

459
00:39:06,441 --> 00:39:08,340
There are those who believe it should never

460
00:39:08,341 --> 00:39:10,040
have been built there.

461
00:39:10,041 --> 00:39:14,920
It should have been built to the west in the Qatara depression.

462
00:39:14,921 --> 00:39:19,480
But Nasser wanted it there, and there it was.

463
00:39:19,481 --> 00:39:23,120
One of the principal negative effects has been the absence of silt.

464
00:39:23,121 --> 00:39:30,400
The agricultural land is no longer covered by the deposits of silt that used to come

465
00:39:30,401 --> 00:39:31,960
with the floods.

466
00:39:31,961 --> 00:39:38,120
As a result, Egyptian farmers have to depend much more extensively on chemical fertilizers,

467
00:39:38,121 --> 00:39:40,780
which have had some very negative effects in the first years.

468
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:46,200
The silt is lying at the bottom of the dam of Lake Nasser.

469
00:39:46,680 --> 00:39:49,500
That silt can be used to fertilize the whole of Egypt.

470
00:39:51,200 --> 00:39:55,660
And it's also decreasing the amount of water that's being kept in the dam

471
00:39:55,661 --> 00:39:58,400
because it's raising the bed of Lake Nasser.

472
00:39:59,160 --> 00:40:04,920
If somebody could invent a way to remove that silt and to use it,

473
00:40:05,720 --> 00:40:08,080
then Egypt wouldn't need chemical fertilizers.

474
00:40:08,120 --> 00:40:14,680
The greatest criticism of the dam is the massive loss of water from evaporation that you have

475
00:40:14,681 --> 00:40:20,240
from this huge 350-mile reservoir.

476
00:40:20,241 --> 00:40:29,180
But it no longer makes Egypt the hostage to unfriendly, hostile, and unstable states upstream.

477
00:40:29,181 --> 00:40:35,820
That is the bottom line, and that is what makes all the criticism of the dam, no matter

478
00:40:35,821 --> 00:40:39,420
how valid, but for the Egyptians, irrelevant.

479
00:40:41,780 --> 00:40:45,420
Today, Egypt continues to look far ahead into her future.

480
00:40:46,220 --> 00:40:51,180
There is already a highly ambitious project underway to create a man-made branch of the Nile.

481
00:40:51,540 --> 00:40:57,100
Starting from the village of Tashka on the shore of Lake Nasser, the new channel would parallel the River Nile.

482
00:40:58,320 --> 00:40:59,820
Oh, it's a monumental investment.

483
00:40:59,821 --> 00:41:02,820
It'll cost more than the high dam and the Suez Canal put together.

484
00:41:05,820 --> 00:41:10,620
The Tashka project is considered the dream of the future and the coming generations,

485
00:41:10,621 --> 00:41:14,860
because the majority of the Egyptians live in the regions of the valley and the delta.

486
00:41:15,900 --> 00:41:18,300
There is a real population crisis.

487
00:41:19,020 --> 00:41:24,140
There is a pressing need to transfer some of these dense populated areas of the valley

488
00:41:24,141 --> 00:41:27,020
and the delta to places outside these areas.

489
00:41:29,340 --> 00:41:32,700
Egypt in the future has two lines, not only one.

490
00:41:32,701 --> 00:41:35,040
has two valleys, not only one.

491
00:41:36,060 --> 00:41:37,880
The other valley, actually, or the other Nile,

492
00:41:37,940 --> 00:41:39,360
actually is starting from Toshka,

493
00:41:40,260 --> 00:41:43,740
going to the oasis and all the way to the Mediterranean.

494
00:41:44,260 --> 00:41:45,480
And between the two Niles,

495
00:41:46,000 --> 00:41:50,960
you can again establish another civilization of Egypt.

496
00:41:52,020 --> 00:41:54,840
Once you give the desert water and fertilizer,

497
00:41:55,180 --> 00:41:58,600
then it blooms, and you can have a second Nile Valley

498
00:41:58,601 --> 00:42:01,200
if this project comes to fruition.

499
00:42:01,201 --> 00:42:05,211
And therefore you could feed twice as many as you do today.

500
00:42:05,212 --> 00:42:08,406
You wouldn't need to import wheat from America.

501
00:42:08,407 --> 00:42:16,020
You would have your own wheat, you would have your own food, and you could even supply Africa with food as well.

502
00:42:16,021 --> 00:42:23,240
Looking back at Egypt's struggles over the last century to feed and accommodate its exploding

503
00:42:23,241 --> 00:42:28,840
population, it seems little more than a dream that this nation once called the breadbasket

504
00:42:28,841 --> 00:42:32,040
of civilization might again take on that role.

505
00:42:32,700 --> 00:42:38,040
But if history proves anything about the Egyptians, it's that they are survivors and they are not

506
00:42:38,041 --> 00:42:39,720
daunted by large projects.

507
00:42:39,721 --> 00:42:44,420
I think Egyptians like to build monumental things.

508
00:42:44,421 --> 00:42:46,420
It goes back to the pharaohs.

509
00:42:47,860 --> 00:42:50,900
Gamal Abdel Nasser died in 1970

510
00:42:50,901 --> 00:42:53,960
without ever seeing the Aswan High Dam completed.

511
00:42:55,180 --> 00:42:58,080
Nasser's funeral was an unbelievable event

512
00:42:58,081 --> 00:43:00,780
where a million Egyptians showed up in the streets of Cairo

513
00:43:00,781 --> 00:43:03,720
to mourn his death.

514
00:43:06,700 --> 00:43:09,640
It was ironic that, in a sense, six months later,

515
00:43:10,320 --> 00:43:14,040
the Aswan High Dam was opened by Anwar Sadat,

516
00:43:14,041 --> 00:43:15,188
his successor.

517
00:43:15,189 --> 00:43:21,340
What exists today as a lasting commemoration of Gamal Abdel Nasser's effort

518
00:43:21,341 --> 00:43:26,340
is principally the fact that the lake, which is the largest lake in Egypt and one of the

519
00:43:26,341 --> 00:43:30,140
largest lakes in Africa, and indeed one of the largest artificial lakes in the world,

520
00:43:30,560 --> 00:43:35,360
is called Lake Nasser. So Nasser's name will never be forgotten from this project.

521
00:43:37,500 --> 00:43:43,920
Thirty years after completion, the high dam is taken for granted by many Egyptians not old enough

522
00:43:43,921 --> 00:43:46,200
to remember the effects of drought or flood.

523
00:43:48,120 --> 00:43:51,740
But each of their lives has been greatly affected by the dam.

524
00:43:54,400 --> 00:43:57,400
It has become, as Nasser often called it,

525
00:43:58,600 --> 00:44:00,520
a pyramid for the living.

526
00:44:07,740 --> 00:44:10,380
No structure in the history of the world

527
00:44:10,381 --> 00:44:12,140
has ever changed a nation

528
00:44:12,141 --> 00:44:17,000
as much as the Aswan High Dam has changed Egypt.

529
00:44:19,080 --> 00:44:21,620
Get in here, truth! Listen up, people!

530
00:44:21,840 --> 00:44:22,940
Who has the best show?

